{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Biological evolution is blind and non-rational, whereas scientific preference is a conscious critical decision.","In biology, 'fitness' equals reproductive success; in science, 'corroboration' equals survival of falsification attempts, which are distinct logical categories.","Evolution has no goal of truth; science explicitly aims at verisimilitude even if unattainable."],"overturn_conditions":["If it can be shown that acting on a theory logically entails an assumption of its future reliability.","If Popper's own writings admit that pragmatic preference secretly relies on the uniformity of nature."],"shared_structure":["Selection of an instrument based on past performance without assuming future consistency.","Distinction between 'fitness for current environment' and 'guarantee of survival'.","Preference driven by information richness rather than safety."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_003","SRC_007"],"transfer_claims":["Just as one uses a map that hasn't failed yet without believing it is perfect, scientists use corroborated theories without believing them true.","The 'fittest' theory is simply the one currently offering the most leverage for discovery, not the one closest to final truth."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's own terms not as an inductive inference of future truth, but as a non-inductive, critical rational choice to adopt the theory with the highest 'verisimilitude-likeness' (content and testability) for the purpose of further criticism, accepting the cost that this preference offers no guarantee of practical success or proximity to truth.","counterconditions":["If the agent requires a probabilistic guarantee of future success to act, the Popperian defense fails.","If 'preference' is defined strictly as 'assigning higher subjective probability,' the defense collapses into induction.","If the context demands a theo